-solution "We compute the differential using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y,z) & =\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x+\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\right)\\,\\text{d}y+\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\right)\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(yz)\\,\\text{d}x+{x}^{2}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(yz)\\,\\text{d}y+{x}^{2}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(yz)\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348270 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "393a2da4-2037-5f88-af20-25566e93ee10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,\\text{d}a+10\\,\\text{d}b+2\\,\\text{d}c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345839} {:answer/id #uuid "5f766058-5345-504f-969f-c6f41943d337" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,\\text{d}a+5\\,\\text{d}b+10\\,\\text{d}c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346176} {:answer/id #uuid "8c626268-e771-514e-9e16-2f37c28e6a23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{d}a+5\\,\\text{d}b+4\\,\\text{d}c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346570} {:answer/id #uuid "bd042560-3b82-573a-bb03-933f8a2d73a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{d}a+2\\,\\text{d}b+10\\,\\text{d}c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346994} {:answer/id #uuid "dbe3e38f-3a94-535d-a8d4-214ff917e54f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{d}a+25\\,\\text{d}b+4\\,\\text{d}c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347278}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd042560-3b82-573a-bb03-933f8a2d73a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{d}a+2\\,\\text{d}b+10\\,\\text{d}c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346994} :answer-field/id #uuid "001e58e4-393c-54c0-893b-8d317963f75b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347603}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eb226681-0351-549f-b81b-c5875e8a6018" :question/math-academy-id "q-79746" :question/problem "Given a rectangular solid, find the differential $\\text{d}V$ for the volume $V$ of the rectangular solid if the lengths of the sides are $a=2,$ $b=5,$ and $c=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The volume of the rectangular solid is $V=abc.$ Let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}V(a,b,c) & =\\left(\\frac{∂V}{∂a}\\right)\\,\\text{d}a+\\left(\\frac{∂V}{∂b}\\right)\\,\\text{d}b+\\left(\\frac{∂V}{∂c}\\right)\\,\\text{d}c \\\\  & =bc\\,\\text{d}a+ac\\,\\text{d}b+ab\\,\\text{d}c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $a=2,$ $b=5,$ and $c=1,$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}V(2,5,1) & =5\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}a+2\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}b+5\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}c \\\\  & =5\\,\\text{d}a+2\\,\\text{d}b+10\\,\\text{d}c.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348271 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "009c9900-f889-52f9-b00b-40100c9397c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40\\,\\text{d}a+12\\,\\text{d}h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345359} {:answer/id #uuid "33de2cd4-781a-5e11-9d06-401da624c0ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17\\,\\text{d}a+12\\,\\text{d}h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345803} {:answer/id #uuid "6c775b28-e659-58c3-afb4-970c5a4d0320" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,\\text{d}a+24\\,\\text{d}h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346292} {:answer/id #uuid "80dabf23-d4b2-59f9-93af-a8e0472606a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40\\,\\text{d}a+48\\,\\text{d}h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346477} {:answer/id #uuid "ef0dac21-38b0-572f-851b-fdb97e5e86a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20\\,\\text{d}a+24\\,\\text{d}h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347461}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "009c9900-f889-52f9-b00b-40100c9397c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40\\,\\text{d}a+12\\,\\text{d}h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345359} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0653cbc-f79b-58c9-bc1b-48709188cc90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347942}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6f0e792d-8d7d-5fba-9784-ec8ac9d90e29" :question/math-academy-id "q-79747" :question/problem "Given a rectangular solid, find the differential $\\text{d}S$ for the surface area $S$ of the rectangular solid if the base is a square with the side of $a=3$ and the height is $h=7.$" :question/worked-solution "The surface area of the given rectangular solid is $S=2{a}^{2}+4ah.$ Let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}S(a,h) & =\\left(\\frac{∂S}{∂a}\\right)\\,\\text{d}a+\\left(\\frac{∂S}{∂h}\\right)\\,\\text{d}h \\\\  & =(4a+4h)\\,\\text{d}a+4a\\,\\text{d}h\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $a=3$ and $h=7,$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}S(3,7) & =(4\\cdot 3+4\\cdot 7)\\,\\text{d}a+4\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}h \\\\  & =40\\,\\text{d}a+12\\,\\text{d}h.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348276 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b63f23d-3741-54dc-9da9-b5e686f502a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{1}{y}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2y+{x}^{2}}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345735} {:answer/id #uuid "50784efb-ac52-5b2a-930e-224a3c46e1de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{1}{y}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346039} {:answer/id #uuid "5e7e5b5f-00cc-57b5-b9a0-eabad58dd3f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}+3x}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346165} {:answer/id #uuid "a42c961b-9393-58eb-a38e-3ff82db15d10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346775} {:answer/id #uuid "f7e532ee-210c-54c5-b6ba-20b24eb7144c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{1}{y}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{y-x}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347547}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b63f23d-3741-54dc-9da9-b5e686f502a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{1}{y}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2y+{x}^{2}}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345735} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6bd8f24-231f-5b7f-ad71-f5ad51631cdd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347926}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c92a3ce0-666c-5499-94f8-c31ec02af3a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-79754" :question/problem "Which of the following is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nLet's attempt to write $\\left(\\frac{1}{y}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2y+{x}^{2}}{y}$ in the standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left(\\frac{1}{y}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2y+{x}^{2}}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y\\left(\\frac{2y+{x}^{2}}{y}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2y+{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y & ={x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is in standard form with  $P(x)=-2$ and $Q(x)={x}^{2}.$ Since the equation is written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations cannot be written in the standard form, and therefore they are not first-order linear ODEs."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348287 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7bf48df2-dc34-52ca-93a9-b4daed0d2e97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346435} {:answer/id #uuid "8429f3a8-0088-5774-8b2b-6ec526d65653" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346501} {:answer/id #uuid "92ce89f6-9755-5e6f-839c-c0fe25166097" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346635} {:answer/id #uuid "a5c7c1da-a44a-587a-bb11-6f4446367efa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346798} {:answer/id #uuid "d5c5f6c8-cebe-5787-bdab-e969594c0fc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347231}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5c7c1da-a44a-587a-bb11-6f4446367efa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346798} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e64129a-5def-586b-ba54-9ee7a632757d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347769}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "660c5793-fb22-5668-8662-5f26917785f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-79809" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,$ use the differential $\\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y)$ from $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ to $(1.5,1.5).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x+\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\right)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}x+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =1\\,\\text{d}x+0\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(2+Δx,\\frac{\\pi }{2}+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈1\\,Δx+0\\,Δy.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ to $(1.5,1.5),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =1.5-2=-0.5, \\\\ Δy & =1.5-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈1\\,Δx+0\\,Δy \\\\  & ≈1\\cdot (-0.5)+0\\cdot (1.5-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & ≈-0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y)$ decreases by approximately $0.5.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348289 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "434f2e6c-b51e-56e2-b3b6-bfea95031930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345933} {:answer/id #uuid "4357d5f4-8f26-5f42-b673-2b35c8637752" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345936} {:answer/id #uuid "45342e19-03d6-5c5e-82c8-aa1f3a9c2881" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345952} {:answer/id #uuid "6975986f-f6e6-55f7-9e5c-13e49e2e5623" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346267} {:answer/id #uuid "d185e4fb-7b96-564b-a235-1e5d04c537b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347188}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45342e19-03d6-5c5e-82c8-aa1f3a9c2881" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345952} :answer-field/id #uuid "922aa6d1-ce19-5aa5-aed9-a049d29daf39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347860}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b3a7a619-a349-551d-ae22-16b086347f4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-79810" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y+\\sqrt{y},$ use the differential $\\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y)$ from $(0,4)$ to $(-1,5).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x+\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\right)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2xy\\,\\text{d}x+({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}})\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(0,4),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(0,4) & =2\\cdot 0\\cdot 4\\,\\text{d}x+({0}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4}})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =0\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(0+Δx,4+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈0\\,Δx+\\frac{1}{4}\\,Δy.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(0,4)$ to $(-1,5),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =-1-0=-1, \\\\ Δy & =5-4=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈0\\,Δx+\\frac{1}{4}\\,Δy \\\\  & ≈0\\cdot (-1)+\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (1) \\\\  & ≈0.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y)$ increases by approximately $0.25.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348290 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b1c3106-c55d-552d-9da9-6400cdf3bf71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345437} {:answer/id #uuid "3eac3844-b3a7-5631-9f41-1f4c730bd68c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345885} {:answer/id #uuid "99e7f39a-7e2a-5658-8f7e-97a1901a556d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346693} {:answer/id #uuid "b0c777f6-4844-5a67-b6d3-ab6691e6fff6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346894} {:answer/id #uuid "dde68c61-4539-56ba-9f5c-926429473a7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347301}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dde68c61-4539-56ba-9f5c-926429473a7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347301} :answer-field/id #uuid "47fc52d4-7652-5ec8-a7a9-cdc987d28e3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347722}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b4eb830a-39de-5829-9892-2282b1954159" :question/math-academy-id "q-79811" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,$ use the differential $\\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y)$ from $(0.5,1)$ to $(0,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x+\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\right)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2x\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(0.5,1),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(0.5,1) & =2\\cdot 0.5\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =1\\,\\text{d}x+1\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(0.5+Δx,1+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈1\\,Δx+1\\,Δy.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(0.5,1)$ to $(0,2),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =0-0.5=-0.5, \\\\ Δy & =2-1=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈1\\,Δx+1\\,Δy \\\\  & ≈1\\cdot (-0.5)+1\\cdot (1) \\\\  & ≈0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y)$ increases by approximately $0.5.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348291 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20b1b01d-dcce-5e97-8070-3e46cd24a9b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345641} {:answer/id #uuid "5d489c63-3bed-55d1-88eb-6a7cb5ad4096" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346150} {:answer/id #uuid "702fd59b-e266-554c-9f4d-07da9651143b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346329} {:answer/id #uuid "c8ef46c8-495f-50a4-88cb-4b955b1c8d64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347101} {:answer/id #uuid "e60a87d9-0bac-5840-9059-0b6443f4f86e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347379}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8ef46c8-495f-50a4-88cb-4b955b1c8d64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347101} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9750bae-5c66-5b82-a422-b4ec85ac0ea9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348011}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a59577cc-4986-5576-9592-2ad42c96ff6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-79812" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,$ use the differential $\\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y)$ from $(0,e)$ to $(1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x+\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\right)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{x}{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(0,e),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(0,e) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡e\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{0}{e}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =1\\,\\text{d}x+0\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(0+Δx,e+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈1\\,Δx+0\\,Δy.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(0,e)$ to $(1,2),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =1-0=1, \\\\ Δy & =2-e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈1\\,Δx+0\\,Δy \\\\  & ≈1\\cdot (1)+0\\cdot (2-e) \\\\  & ≈1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y)$ increases by approximately $1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348292 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d5f0ff3-67c3-5156-80d4-3b1056e54271" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345456} {:answer/id #uuid "aa5c1f15-75a4-5090-ab69-ed2b1d6b4397" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346839} {:answer/id #uuid "b34d8f8b-645c-5063-9782-d98391879677" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346918} {:answer/id #uuid "c7663d80-ea3d-53cf-9cff-cd7f8bff08ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347085} {:answer/id #uuid "de53245c-33e1-5dc2-9980-d68bbba16eee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347307}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d5f0ff3-67c3-5156-80d4-3b1056e54271" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345456} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d29f153-5da4-5bde-bade-0dc96a9e6da3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347802}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "01cbcb4d-5667-5e3e-806c-ef99a5773f2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-79813" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=xy+x{e}^{y},$ use the differential $\\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y)$ from $(2,0)$ to $(1,-0.5).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x+\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\right)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =(y+{e}^{y})\\,\\text{d}x+(x+x{e}^{y})\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(2,0),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(2,0) & =(0+{e}^{0})\\,\\text{d}x+(2+2\\cdot {e}^{0})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =1\\,\\text{d}x+4\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(2+Δx,0+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈1\\,Δx+4\\,Δy.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(2,0)$ to $(1,-0.5),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =1-2=-1, \\\\ Δy & =-0.5-0=-0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈1\\,Δx+4\\,Δy \\\\  & ≈1\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot (-0.5) \\\\  & ≈-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y)$ decreases by approximately $3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348293 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18aa2d29-4200-55a9-9049-eec28d6ce46d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345569} {:answer/id #uuid "28d8915f-3339-5227-a3ee-86c79928eb21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345717} {:answer/id #uuid "39be8076-d1a4-53bc-beb7-69d5faf74820" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345843} {:answer/id #uuid "e580e49f-e01d-5220-aa00-628de33ae96e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347372} {:answer/id #uuid "fb111822-7947-5e96-ad01-3bd29918f4a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347567}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18aa2d29-4200-55a9-9049-eec28d6ce46d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345569} :answer-field/id #uuid "da7d86ef-4dea-5ade-8600-60a413066868" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347990}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0a50b343-0303-5110-8cf9-6975a85c04cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-79815" :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡z,$ use the differential $\\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y,z)$ from $(1,0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ to $(0,1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y,z) & =\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x+\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\right)\\,\\text{d}y+\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}x+(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡z)\\,\\text{d}y-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(1,0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(1,0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)\\,\\text{d}x+(1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))\\,\\text{d}y-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =0\\,\\text{d}x+1\\,\\text{d}y+0\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(1+Δx,0+Δy,\\frac{\\pi }{2}+Δz),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈0\\,Δx+1\\,Δy+0\\,Δz.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(1,0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ to $(0,1,1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =0-1=-1, \\\\ Δy & =1-0=1 \\\\ Δz & =1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈0\\,Δx+1\\,Δy+0\\,Δz \\\\  & ≈0\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot (1)+0\\cdot (1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & ≈1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y,z)$ increases by approximately $1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348300 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "032ef8bf-a149-5f5d-b9bc-54e6d8674bc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.062" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345377} {:answer/id #uuid "3c56bb24-5e3e-5dba-9bd7-0f16e2f3edd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.066" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345861} {:answer/id #uuid "7d3bf950-dbc6-55c7-8257-4238683ae1b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.060" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346447} {:answer/id #uuid "85c38cb7-115d-538e-baa8-9d152e8db340" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.058" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346514} {:answer/id #uuid "8cf00326-ca9e-5146-a772-c3778f9f8641" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.064" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346578}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d3bf950-dbc6-55c7-8257-4238683ae1b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.060" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346447} :answer-field/id #uuid "52c20e21-9157-5114-a78d-535633267fdc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347748}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2e921641-2f51-5af1-85b0-a27e011ac9c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-79836" :question/problem "Estimate $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.12)$ using a differential $\\text{d}f$ of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x+\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\right)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},0),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡0\\,\\text{d}x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡0\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =0\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+Δx,0+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈0\\,Δx+\\frac{1}{2}\\,Δy\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.12)=f(1,0.12),\n$$\n\nso we want to move to the point $(1,0.12).$\n\nMoving from $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},0)$ to $(1,0.12),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =1-\\frac{\\pi }{3}, \\\\ Δy & =0.12-0=0.12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈0\\cdot (1-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (0.12) \\\\  & ≈0+0.5\\cdot 0.12 \\\\  & ≈0.06.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the value of $f$ increases by about $0.06.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0.12) & ≈f(\\frac{\\pi }{3},0)+0.06 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.12) & ≈\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+0.06 \\\\  & ≈0+0.06 \\\\  & ≈0.06.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348301 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03459f86-cd26-56df-bfd3-ae80dccdcdbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.967" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345380} {:answer/id #uuid "0e1e8fae-ee5c-5939-a4ae-b67a16f3db2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.965" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345464} {:answer/id #uuid "1134ab4d-4a2c-5eb6-80d3-c7193d047b90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.961" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345498} {:answer/id #uuid "519e5c86-d04e-53b4-9169-038767ba67c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.970" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346054} {:answer/id #uuid "6af737dd-91d3-51d2-ab28-2ef9af78e57c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.972" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346275}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03459f86-cd26-56df-bfd3-ae80dccdcdbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.967" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345380} :answer-field/id #uuid "f399272f-f649-5a0c-8d81-b24dff62d20e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348033}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3c9a3a87-a14e-5269-82cd-f0a320dbf13f" :question/math-academy-id "q-79839" :question/problem "Estimate the value of ${e}^{\\sqrt{10}-3.2}$ using a differential $\\text{d}f$ of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{\\sqrt{x}-y}$ at the point $(9,3).$ Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x+\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\right)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{x}-y}}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x-{e}^{\\sqrt{x}-y}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(9,3),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{9}-3}}{2\\sqrt{9}}\\,\\text{d}x-{e}^{\\sqrt{9}-3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{0}}{6}\\,\\text{d}x-{e}^{0}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{d}x-1\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(9+Δx,3+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈\\frac{1}{6}\\,Δx-1\\,Δy\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n{e}^{\\sqrt{10}-3.2}=f(10,3.2),\n$$\n\nso we want to move to the point $(10,3.2).$\n\nMoving from $(9,3)$ to $(10,3.2),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =10-9=1, \\\\ Δy & =3.2-3=0.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & ≈0.167-0.2 \\\\  & ≈-0.033.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the value of $f$ decreases by about $0.033.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(10,3.2) & ≈f(9,3)-0.033 \\\\ {e}^{\\sqrt{10}-3.2} & ≈{e}^{\\sqrt{9}-3}-0.033 \\\\  & ≈1-0.033 \\\\  & ≈0.967.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348302 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "234514d9-f9aa-5d5a-a4a5-1d99124c0eb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.910" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345670} {:answer/id #uuid "27b673f1-0512-5eac-a559-c4064771c9b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.930" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345708} {:answer/id #uuid "44b686f0-4915-566f-ad11-e858437bbaa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.920" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345945} {:answer/id #uuid "5b7afac0-8d40-5755-9aae-b92cbfe0ab03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.915" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346132} {:answer/id #uuid "ab85cd85-cd43-5fc1-9828-426b43ab7315" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.925" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab85cd85-cd43-5fc1-9828-426b43ab7315" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.925" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346846} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3d12d8e-fb58-55d0-ac4f-b9ece742f19e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347917}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "54765bb6-f6e1-50b8-8581-fc92ecfe7a38" :question/math-academy-id "q-79840" :question/problem "Estimate $\\sqrt{4.1}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.9)$ using a differential $\\text{d}f$ of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ at the point $(4,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x+\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\right)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(4,1),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}x+1\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(4+Δx,1+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈\\frac{1}{4}\\,Δx+1\\,Δy\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{4.1}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.9)=f(4.1,0.9),\n$$\n\nso we want to move to the point $(4.1,0.9).$\n\nMoving from $(4,1)$ to $(4.1,0.9),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =4.1-4=0.1, \\\\ Δy & =0.9-1=-0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (0.1)+1(-0.1) \\\\  & ≈0.025-0.1 \\\\  & ≈-0.075.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the value of $f$ decreases by about $0.075.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4.1,0.9) & ≈f(4,1)-0.075 \\\\ \\sqrt{4.1}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.9) & ≈\\sqrt{4}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)-0.075 \\\\  & ≈2-0.075 \\\\  & ≈1.925.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348303 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33af41af-086f-50d8-a48a-7d4575264d27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.38" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345800} {:answer/id #uuid "7e3aea50-e997-5fa3-94e5-480d64d9efef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346460} {:answer/id #uuid "80c55faf-5e90-5e00-9f5a-2d7a75d1b206" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346475} {:answer/id #uuid "b2e7c7b9-f7dd-575a-9ff9-6fbb06325d0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346909} {:answer/id #uuid "beb90288-7d21-5b9e-95ab-e8e00071b3e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347002}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "beb90288-7d21-5b9e-95ab-e8e00071b3e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347002} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c9369d6-8a86-5559-a242-8e9e34c0205e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347732}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6467df78-2b9b-5255-9ede-8186a25e3f9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-79841" :question/problem "Estimate $\\sqrt{3.9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.2)$ using a differential $\\text{d}f$ of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ at the point $(4,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x+\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\right)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{y}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(4,1),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡1}{2\\sqrt{4}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{\\sqrt{4}}{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =0\\,\\text{d}x+2\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(4+Δx,1+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈0\\,Δx+2\\,Δy\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3.9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.2)=f(3.9,1.2),\n$$\n\nso we want to move to the point $(3.9,1.2).$\n\nMoving from $(4,1)$ to $(3.9,1.2),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =3.9-4=-0.1, \\\\ Δy & =1.2-1=0.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈0\\cdot (-0.1)+2\\cdot (0.2) \\\\  & ≈0+0.4 \\\\  & ≈0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the value of $f$ increases by about $0.4.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3.9,1.2) & ≈f(4,1)+0.4 \\\\ \\sqrt{3.9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.2) & ≈\\sqrt{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+0.4 \\\\  & ≈0+0.4 \\\\  & ≈0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348304 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5677a6cf-3ae1-5c05-a7ea-d18c541cd27a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346086} {:answer/id #uuid "924a5a3d-80d3-5178-be63-b38474407f0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346630} {:answer/id #uuid "c0638f2c-fe79-54bf-9fa1-4ff52d6c1be9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347015} {:answer/id #uuid "cbfbad30-45da-5493-a15a-e1bb6fe1f0a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347138} {:answer/id #uuid "dc032e4b-974c-59fc-a8c3-0ad4f14b6c8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc032e4b-974c-59fc-a8c3-0ad4f14b6c8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347279} :answer-field/id #uuid "c553bfad-eb56-5870-b224-d71594c0704b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347950}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1639694c-cbca-54ad-bf88-2314edcecfd3" :question/math-academy-id "q-79842" :question/problem "Estimate $2.1{e}^{0.2}$ using a differential $\\text{d}f$ of the function $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}$ at the point $(2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x+\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\right)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x+x{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(2,0),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & ={e}^{0}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\cdot {e}^{0}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =1\\,\\text{d}x+2\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(2+Δx,0+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈1\\,Δx+2\\,Δy\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n2.1{e}^{0.2}=f(2.1,0.2),\n$$\n\nso we want to move to the point $(2.1,0.2).$\n\nMoving from $(2,0)$ to $(2.1,0.2),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =2.1-2=0.1, \\\\ Δy & =0.2-0=0.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈1\\cdot (0.1)+2\\cdot (0.2) \\\\  & ≈0.1+0.4 \\\\  & ≈0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the value of $f$ decreases by about $0.5.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2.1,0.2) & ≈f(2,0)+0.5 \\\\ 2.1{e}^{0.2} & ≈2{e}^{0}+0.5 \\\\  & ≈2+0.5 \\\\  & ≈2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348306 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25aca136-de99-54dd-b945-23c1dcf34a48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345693} {:answer/id #uuid "7beab84d-6621-58f0-affc-ca59f8977bb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346434} {:answer/id #uuid "c41d60d5-79a0-517f-8981-2a0575f5c50c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347046} {:answer/id #uuid "ecea43e1-5c71-5328-a98c-41f1756aade9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347441} {:answer/id #uuid "f7aa9fdb-97ad-5cc8-8020-7fcd4317caef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347545}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7beab84d-6621-58f0-affc-ca59f8977bb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346434} :answer-field/id #uuid "725c2f1b-9770-5882-8e67-da604b7995d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347810}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6749d91f-49a8-5f4a-a9d1-89ed10459b4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-79876" :question/problem "Estimate $(1.06)^{4.1}$ using a differential $\\text{d}f$ of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{y}$ at the point $(1,4).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x+\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\right)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =y{x}^{y-1}\\,\\text{d}x+{x}^{y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(1,4),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =4\\cdot {1}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+{1}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4\\,\\text{d}x+0\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(1+Δx,4+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈4\\,Δx+0\\,Δy.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n(1.06)^{4.1}=f(1.06,4.1),\n$$\n\nso we want to move to the point $(1.06,4.1).$\n\nMoving from $(1,4)$ to $(1.06,4.1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =1.06-1=0.06, \\\\ Δy & =4.1-4=0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈4\\cdot (0.06)+0\\cdot (0.1) \\\\  & ≈0.24+0 \\\\  & ≈0.24\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the value of $f$ increases by about $0.24.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.06,4.1) & ≈f(1,4)+0.24 \\\\ (1.06)^{4.1} & ≈{1}^{4}+0.24 \\\\  & ≈1+0.24 \\\\  & ≈1.24\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348321 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16681232-d875-51d4-8775-995a4bdc038d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\text{d}r+4\\,\\text{d}h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345550} {:answer/id #uuid "19759a8b-a542-5b9d-ac6b-52141e753e0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}\\,\\text{d}r+\\frac{3}{10}\\,\\text{d}h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345575} {:answer/id #uuid "39123486-345d-5915-be9e-6db3c7d3e8ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}\\,\\text{d}r+\\frac{4}{5}\\,\\text{d}h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345838} {:answer/id #uuid "65039564-9812-5d42-8c1d-dcf7c26c5410" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}\\,\\text{d}r+\\frac{2}{5}\\,\\text{d}h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346227} {:answer/id #uuid "d3029df8-972e-585a-af9f-01f6b385376c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}\\,\\text{d}r+\\frac{3}{5}\\,\\text{d}h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347196}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39123486-345d-5915-be9e-6db3c7d3e8ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}\\,\\text{d}r+\\frac{4}{5}\\,\\text{d}h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345838} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7bb7a4f-725e-5075-b3cf-da3a1d65ad3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347902}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "00fbdc66-0e7c-5f09-b062-4832cba6efd9" :question/math-academy-id "q-79958" :question/problem "Given a right cone, find the differential $\\text{d}l$ for the slant height $l$ of the right cone if its base radius is $r=3$ and height $h=4.$" :question/worked-solution "The slant height of the right cone is $l=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{h}^{2}}.$ Let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}l(r,h) & =\\left(\\frac{∂l}{∂r}\\right)\\,\\text{d}r+\\left(\\frac{∂l}{∂h}\\right)\\,\\text{d}h \\\\  & =\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{h}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}r+\\frac{h}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{h}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}h\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $r=3$  and $h=4$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}V(3,4) & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}r+\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}h \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\,\\text{d}r+\\frac{4}{5}\\,\\text{d}h.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348340 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f499e98-f09e-5a6d-9b2a-340d435d2fa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345478} {:answer/id #uuid "4e0eb368-d78c-5627-94c9-735d1191475a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346017} {:answer/id #uuid "9c99ae3a-8e34-5c4f-81eb-b64ac9297706" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346715} {:answer/id #uuid "df2c3b34-e095-50bd-8f01-daadfb608458" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347314} {:answer/id #uuid "fcf16568-06ed-50eb-9100-dffdaf59eab6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fcf16568-06ed-50eb-9100-dffdaf59eab6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347575} :answer-field/id #uuid "9425d25d-3a02-5af6-8eef-f6f9499e68e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "34bb0a32-a0ab-5d0c-a2bf-a7983774c148" :question/math-academy-id "q-80133" :question/problem "Given that the expression $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+10\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ)$ with ${0}^{∘}<ϕ<{90}^{∘}$ and $R>0$, what is the value of $ϕ?$" :question/worked-solution "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+10\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+10\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ &=2 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ &=10\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{10}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =5 \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348343 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77dc6fdd-2db7-55bf-abb5-b59442a1464c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346402} {:answer/id #uuid "b477c45c-b737-549b-91b5-d1091460ac32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346923} {:answer/id #uuid "d114ac37-9962-5408-9d6e-05d942670183" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347182} {:answer/id #uuid "e341ede8-046b-52c2-b8b6-aa4cf9fe8ca2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347350} {:answer/id #uuid "fe903f8c-2fae-5ab6-9e3a-9928ee1b850e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347588}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d114ac37-9962-5408-9d6e-05d942670183" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347182} :answer-field/id #uuid "6df7bc06-d4ba-5a57-ab49-fe4b9ffa350a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347804}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b478a4e0-e373-5d4c-8f17-c97eaae0c7f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-80136" :question/problem "Given that the expression $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ)$ with ${0}^{∘}<ϕ<{90}^{∘}$ and $R>0$, what is the value of $ϕ?$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ &=2 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ &=8\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{8}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =4 \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348351 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31d958e9-9f9f-5b1d-bc41-4d3dd58e5ca9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345790} {:answer/id #uuid "8c8b0321-4d4c-5f39-b679-08dae3252e7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346572} {:answer/id #uuid "f28b8efa-6893-5c5c-ba05-872ce4539c7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347498} {:answer/id #uuid "f9e0db95-6549-5c86-b456-4bc95a71e74e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347557} {:answer/id #uuid "fbf25099-e210-548b-9616-1266388c9228" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347571}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9e0db95-6549-5c86-b456-4bc95a71e74e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347557} :answer-field/id #uuid "53205bfc-33e4-52dd-9b65-e461aded3334" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347753}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "708ed8ba-e035-53b7-9300-ab76a887742c" :question/math-academy-id "q-80193" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $4=4-\\frac{3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{2}$ for $-2\\pi <x<0,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =4-\\frac{3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{2} \\\\ 0 & =-\\frac{3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can't find an equivalent equation involving tangent, since this would lead to us dividing by $0:$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{0}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can instead find an equivalent equation using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=0\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can find the principal solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ lies outside the given domain, it is not a solution.\n\nTo find more solutions, we subtract integer multiples of $\\pi $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-\\pi +x=-\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2} & =-2\\pi +x=-2\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{2}=-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our solutions are $x=-\\frac{3\\pi }{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\left(-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348352 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b587d7e-085f-5513-8ee4-71450c89f462" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345998} {:answer/id #uuid "5dff2710-b06a-5b8a-8fb4-be6ff1580df2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346158} {:answer/id #uuid "9720cc12-6f5d-5646-a463-8ffc9a78c78c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346674} {:answer/id #uuid "c1a82249-3f09-5b69-90dc-c552d0211ad7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347025} {:answer/id #uuid "e312b825-4501-5dcc-8c5d-c04a56844fc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347348}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9720cc12-6f5d-5646-a463-8ffc9a78c78c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346674} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a0fa468-2458-5758-8085-02a20d42b3b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347792}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "82240314-e152-5274-978a-b5eee6a6b692" :question/math-academy-id "q-80200" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $7\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-5=-5-3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ for $-\\pi <x<\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-5 & =-5-3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\ 10\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can't find an equivalent equation involving tangent, since this would lead to us dividing by $0:$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{0}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can instead find an equivalent equation using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=0\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can find the principal solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value lies inside the desired domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTo find more solutions, we subtract integer multiples of $\\pi $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our solutions are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348359 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e292fd4-3a6d-5ea9-bc60-eb76a02ac7f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{arcsec}(x+2)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346159} {:answer/id #uuid "65d16fde-472a-5454-8885-c24fadefed86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{arcsec}\\left(\\frac{x+1}{2}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346238} {:answer/id #uuid "794e0cf8-9e4e-5f4f-a1ec-e99f27abb154" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{arcsec}(x+1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346413} {:answer/id #uuid "916efffa-9fa6-558d-b257-5b948a3e436a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(x+1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346622} {:answer/id #uuid "a2ad6372-a008-5d03-89b1-3818438a388e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(x+2)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346762}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "794e0cf8-9e4e-5f4f-a1ec-e99f27abb154" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{arcsec}(x+1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346413} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a5ac89a-0b95-54ab-a1f6-007eed434a51" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f140ef15-884a-5848-bea0-7026afe13f1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-80312" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{2}{|x+1|\\sqrt{(x+1)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{|x+1|\\sqrt{(x+1)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{|x+1|\\sqrt{(x+1)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse secant:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}(u)+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(x+1).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution \n\n$$\nu=x+1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{|x+1|\\sqrt{(x+1)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{|x+1|\\sqrt{(x+1)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\,\\text{arcsec}(u)+C \\\\  & =2\\,\\text{arcsec}(x+1)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348362 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c0e7973-2bf9-5f0c-8dd7-5255df51d9ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345447} {:answer/id #uuid "2084e431-7940-5e9b-9aad-cc72a2f7172c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345639} {:answer/id #uuid "bba6104b-7d29-5031-9415-d138f6b6a1e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346979} {:answer/id #uuid "eae52c7e-e8f9-542b-a3b0-ad12b106e8ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347423} {:answer/id #uuid "f02646bf-8450-5c94-8029-3d36d8d998fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347470}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bba6104b-7d29-5031-9415-d138f6b6a1e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346979} :answer-field/id #uuid "7adbbbe8-ab6b-5099-bc13-486b0b5c1e7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347823}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4d98964f-3df0-5437-bc4a-3bb084da1d43" :question/math-academy-id "q-80324" :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda =2$ and $\\lambda =-1$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\left(-\\frac{5}{2}\\right){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For $\\lambda =-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{bmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get the eigenvector\n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348368 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cae36fd-3deb-5b7b-8b36-9f9f8dfee127" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345599} {:answer/id #uuid "583455da-d6e1-5029-a452-b73277cbbf12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346105} {:answer/id #uuid "613d7756-c42b-5e58-8499-7e22134bae89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346194} {:answer/id #uuid "7b39e153-c534-5ace-84e4-fe8c0f3ea308" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346428} {:answer/id #uuid "a987b942-fa10-5bee-b0d4-7db1e3099b24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346830}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a987b942-fa10-5bee-b0d4-7db1e3099b24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346830} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fc125c1-0b75-5549-98a7-4582698ca44e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347776}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bc51916f-5f62-552c-9209-e6b4fd87da10" :question/math-academy-id "q-80347" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{8-{x}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=8-{x}^{3}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,-\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u={x}^{2}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=8-{x}^{3}$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & -1 & -\\infty  \\\\ u & 9 & \\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{8-{x}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{\\infty }^{9}\\left(-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{u}}\\right)\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{9}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{8-{x}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{9}^{a}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[2\\sqrt{u}]}_{9}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\sqrt{a}-\\sqrt{9}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\infty  \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348377 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06f49280-3e27-52c2-ba02-a96c2c902002" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}){e}^{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345412} {:answer/id #uuid "148b0fbb-3fc3-56e6-af34-b7980a1c68e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4y{x}^{2}{e}^{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345527} {:answer/id #uuid "25a65378-ebdd-5865-99bb-e4f2fc2f3666" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x(1+2{y}^{2}){e}^{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345692} {:answer/id #uuid "55cd2ed6-cc04-5772-bc87-841e5b1d916f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y(1+2{x}^{2}){e}^{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346080} {:answer/id #uuid "e74f07be-2559-5476-9d96-1dec0cdc625d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4y(1+{x}^{2}){e}^{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347391}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55cd2ed6-cc04-5772-bc87-841e5b1d916f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y(1+2{x}^{2}){e}^{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346080} :answer-field/id #uuid "b013f07d-ae92-5419-a127-b78331669dac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347908}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fa43a83f-a97a-54b7-8060-e07ac7e5d1a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-80396" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y),$ calculate $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}$ given that $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2xy{e}^{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy{e}^{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}) \\\\  & =2y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}) \\\\  & =2y\\cdot ({e}^{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}+2{x}^{2}{e}^{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}) \\\\  & =2y(1+2{x}^{2}){e}^{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348378 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b7ae8a1-ebe2-59c4-8708-e4a1c32b175a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4xy}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345857} {:answer/id #uuid "7d3485ae-94f3-5b77-9f2e-002df7361ef2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x-y}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346445} {:answer/id #uuid "87a67059-8153-5a03-ae43-6b7e24dceadd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346526} {:answer/id #uuid "a468da5e-c9b4-5727-a6a0-5c7197bd864d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4xy}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346778} {:answer/id #uuid "ff97d500-0898-5d7e-a963-3b72cb011bdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{-(x+y)}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347597}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87a67059-8153-5a03-ae43-6b7e24dceadd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346526} :answer-field/id #uuid "a24c8cfd-e105-5686-9a9f-4680bc4eac94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347893}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "afef13bf-72ea-50e0-9df2-d85aa9be21e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-80403" :question/problem "Find ${f}_{xx}(x,y)+{f}_{yy}(x,y)$ given that  ${f}_{x}(x,y)=-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}_{xx}(x,y)$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =-y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =-y\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{2x}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{2xy}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find ${f}_{yy}(x,y)$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =x\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{2y}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2xy}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y)+{f}_{yy}(x,y) & =\\frac{2xy}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}-\\frac{2xy}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348384 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03b02459-8a33-5b66-ac6c-e0cfa3de124c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345384} {:answer/id #uuid "18016af6-5731-5f22-ae66-854878661afa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4x}{({x}^{2}+y)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345563} {:answer/id #uuid "5857a9d0-befa-530e-9e5e-06a8b3da0ab6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4x}{{x}^{2}+y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346108} {:answer/id #uuid "bb8e90d4-2f23-575e-b83d-426558039ecf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{({x}^{2}+y)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346977} {:answer/id #uuid "f44429e7-a1bb-5986-9d6b-37df9f3bc3e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x+1}{({x}^{2}+y)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347518}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18016af6-5731-5f22-ae66-854878661afa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4x}{({x}^{2}+y)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345563} :answer-field/id #uuid "698e83a6-8f4d-51ad-9085-8f734c724561" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347789}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0067d163-3060-585d-9cac-2338577d165d" :question/math-academy-id "q-80456" :question/problem "Find ${f}_{xy}(x,y)+{f}_{yx}(x,y)$ given that  ${f}_{x}(x,y)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+y}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+y}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}_{xy}(x,y)$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+y}\\right) \\\\  & =2x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+y}\\right) \\\\  & =2x\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}+y)^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x}{({x}^{2}+y)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find ${f}_{yx}(x,y)$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+y}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}+y)^{2}}\\right)\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x}{({x}^{2}+y)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y)+{f}_{yx}(x,y) & =-\\frac{2x}{({x}^{2}+y)^{2}}-\\frac{2x}{({x}^{2}+y)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4x}{({x}^{2}+y)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348392 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9249f12b-8f47-54df-837c-46bd00940c7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(y-x)^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346629} {:answer/id #uuid "9c6e2170-8556-58b4-87e5-b4342d8c0780" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}-2xy-{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346713} {:answer/id #uuid "a342f46f-5d1b-5d59-bfa9-4ce45440986e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}+2xy-{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346765} {:answer/id #uuid "c254751b-5f52-5f60-ae60-75a146d27f19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347032} {:answer/id #uuid "dc75be38-3e79-58e8-9cfd-f6f81ed93ca1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}-2{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347282}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c6e2170-8556-58b4-87e5-b4342d8c0780" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}-2xy-{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346713} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0bdb74f-77f3-598a-98e0-1511f72baad7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347943}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3cbdca8a-7538-57ef-93a7-e4f6f9084490" :question/math-academy-id "q-80469" :question/problem "Find ${g}_{xy}(x,y)+{g}_{xx}(x,y)$ given that  ${g}_{x}(x,y)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${g}_{xy}(x,y)$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative ${g}_{x}(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({g}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =x\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{2y}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2xy}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find ${g}_{xx}(x,y)$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative ${g}_{x}(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{xx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({g}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-\\frac{x}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{xy}(x,y)+{g}_{xx}(x,y) & =-\\frac{2xy}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}-2xy-{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348394 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02a5c262-cc22-5983-a61f-511098951290" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345372} {:answer/id #uuid "9fa8ba48-0f15-5b2c-84b8-a833da307fbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346746} {:answer/id #uuid "a9910791-e486-5fd3-bb75-79cd1d4e8819" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346831} {:answer/id #uuid "ae3d5ea0-19f6-5122-82a9-c2add49ba24b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$E$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346871} {:answer/id #uuid "dfb53f77-ac95-5079-afcd-085b46b877ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347317}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9910791-e486-5fd3-bb75-79cd1d4e8819" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346831} :answer-field/id #uuid "530dc008-9954-52d4-b786-4fab0747c350" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347751}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "050f4d1f-7ad3-50e3-a5b8-1b02cf764a27" :question/math-academy-id "q-80480" :question/problem "Find matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=E,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix and $E=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ -6 & -8\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm. \n\n**Step 1.** We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix $E$ using its characteristic equation $det(E-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}7-\\lambda  & 9 \\\\ -6 & -8-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )(-8-\\lambda )-9\\cdot (-6) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -2 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $E$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $E.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E-I & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ -6 & -8\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 9 \\\\ -6 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 9 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -9 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E-(-2)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ -6 & -8\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 9 \\\\ -6 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 9 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & ={R}_{2}+\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348395 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11280adc-f2f4-556c-b831-fa0db135b14c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and\n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345497} {:answer/id #uuid "1ffa557c-011d-5935-9b8b-5915bca53803" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and\n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345632} {:answer/id #uuid "7c960909-d4c7-5e14-a91b-458618e40e5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and\n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346438} {:answer/id #uuid "a4362130-7316-50c8-8cb6-e9d65c6826e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and\n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346776} {:answer/id #uuid "e1f20dbd-46e0-518a-8886-732ddab5d7c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and\n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347334}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1f20dbd-46e0-518a-8886-732ddab5d7c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and\n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347334} :answer-field/id #uuid "28c2da63-7f18-51f5-bf93-3312d17f056a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347670}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cac09b37-360a-5efb-9630-b76644cac8f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-80481" :question/problem "Find matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=B,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix and $B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.** We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix $B$ using its characteristic equation $det(B-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda )-2\\cdot (-1) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda +1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $B$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ \n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $B.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-0I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}-0\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-(-1)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & ={R}_{2}+\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348401 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45813fa4-fac0-5452-9255-9d2cc5a09b20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345956} {:answer/id #uuid "5837a18a-ae8a-5078-b71f-c3ebdbb5af5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346106} {:answer/id #uuid "92622851-e5da-5c25-8c6c-1a4734ebd54b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346632} {:answer/id #uuid "ac9f3b3e-4a16-5638-8f30-348e6dacf30a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346857} {:answer/id #uuid "acd4a2a8-18a1-55b8-81a7-2cf19b948e4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346858}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45813fa4-fac0-5452-9255-9d2cc5a09b20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345956} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b7b34a4-1322-5681-a0fb-3dfd4c852dbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347731}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "813cfc16-7a47-56e2-b9f6-16d7ae4bba08" :question/math-academy-id "q-80507" :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral ${∬}_{R}\\frac{1}{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A$ over the rectangle $R=[1,e]\\times [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the multiplicative property of double integrals and then evaluate each of the integrals separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}\\frac{1}{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{R}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =({\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]_{1}^{e}\\cdot [\\operatorname{sin}⁡y]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348403 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00055a40-3e21-5f4f-a9ae-8d1688e527ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}(e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345353} {:answer/id #uuid "577deb61-f24d-590a-9807-13b42db9a138" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}(1-e)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346097} {:answer/id #uuid "647b5985-fcd8-58b4-ac61-9e2283ed4e31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{12}(1-e)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346225} {:answer/id #uuid "6680f176-e117-5c27-8103-6a030ff928cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}(e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346248} {:answer/id #uuid "cbb026b9-57b7-5eba-91d7-8fb9a18d7d4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{12}(e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347135}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cbb026b9-57b7-5eba-91d7-8fb9a18d7d4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{12}(e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347135} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb525b7c-de79-5a04-b4d0-b9d8f22d4461" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347957}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1f2343bf-e2e3-54a4-85a0-c7ec7325e159" :question/math-academy-id "q-80511" :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral ${∬}_{R}\\frac{{e}^{y}}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A$ over the rectangle $R=[1,\\sqrt{3}]\\times [0,1].$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the multiplicative property of double integrals and then evaluate each of the integrals separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}\\frac{{e}^{y}}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{R}\\left(\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\right)({e}^{y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =({\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x]_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot [{e}^{y}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\sqrt{3}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1)\\cdot ({e}^{1}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot (e-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}(e-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348404 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "099529b2-f2d8-571a-8051-1abe76e80e34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345427} {:answer/id #uuid "1c89dd2c-b2a6-55ce-9cde-64a9db39f22a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345598} {:answer/id #uuid "2af3437a-013c-5c2c-ba66-e91d3d0b3776" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345732} {:answer/id #uuid "e08432ed-6cab-5f33-bc9d-ccb5e07f25a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347321} {:answer/id #uuid "e22f899c-983b-59f9-b450-dbd732e6a167" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347340}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2af3437a-013c-5c2c-ba66-e91d3d0b3776" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345732} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f5bdbcf-f249-5c99-b7cb-71c1723c6391" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347774}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "19bd97de-146f-5b50-9592-8b78d39231d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-80523" :question/problem "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s$, where\n\n$$\nr(s)=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right),\\,5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right)⟩,\\,0\\le s<10\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $T(s)$ denote the unit tangent to the given curve as a function of the arc length $s.$ Since we are given the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖=‖{r}^{″}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(s)$ and ${r}^{″}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{5},\\,5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right)⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{5},\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}⟩ \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(s) & =‖{r}^{″}(s)‖ \\\\  & =|-\\frac{1}{5}|\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right))}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right)+{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348420 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0abddb8f-91f8-5ee8-9e90-e4eb6046e101" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345435} {:answer/id #uuid "17002d3c-d0d8-5485-aa3f-3748e9b1113a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345554} {:answer/id #uuid "63123636-56ca-5c3f-9905-b24651986635" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346214} {:answer/id #uuid "cb7edda9-0cf7-5d75-bb2c-a28098da660f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347131} {:answer/id #uuid "d6757e65-24a6-5b28-8adf-6e8e535e245a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347241}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63123636-56ca-5c3f-9905-b24651986635" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346214} :answer-field/id #uuid "90af9d53-8403-569d-8a7b-66ffcbfd6f0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347856}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0e0662ae-2a01-57b3-bb86-0addfedab9d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-80607" :question/problem "Consider the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by arc length $s.$ Find the curvature at the point where $s=1$ if the unit tangent vector of the curve is given by\n\n$$\nT(s)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{s}^{2}}}⟨1,s⟩.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature: \n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and evaluate it at $s=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{s}^{2}}}⟨1,s⟩) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{s}^{2}}}\\right),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{1+{s}^{2}}}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{s}{(1+{s}^{2})^{3/2}},\\,\\frac{1}{(1+{s}^{2})^{3/2}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(1+{s}^{2})^{3/2}}⟨-s,1⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{(1+{1}^{2})^{3/2}}⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}⟨-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we obtain the curvature:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(1) & =‖{T}^{′}(1)‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}‖⟨-1,1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348422 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25b07490-2e9d-5382-95ee-13901b538e67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345694} {:answer/id #uuid "704fb07f-236f-5ce0-b712-d6856bafcbd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346331} {:answer/id #uuid "86c5fb92-389a-588d-97e5-85b2d16e150e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346519} {:answer/id #uuid "b656a5c6-a0ef-5bc2-a3f2-627207b184a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346940} {:answer/id #uuid "f3b1a719-60bf-51c9-8f1d-53f7845571f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347508}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86c5fb92-389a-588d-97e5-85b2d16e150e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346519} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3c04455-dc5a-5200-ac66-7d3e2ad49eb3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348002}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "56077b9c-81fb-5eb2-90a5-cf058b4a5d1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-80614" :question/problem "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(t)$ given that its derivative ${r}^{′}(t)$ and the derivative of the corresponding unit tangent ${T}^{′}(t)$ are as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)=\\,i-\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}}\\,j,\\,{T}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{t}{2\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}}\\,i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the derivative of the curve ${r}^{′}(t)$ and the derivative of the unit tangent ${T}^{′}(t)$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the magnitudes of ${r}^{′}(t)$ and ${T}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{\\left(-\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4-{t}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4-{t}^{2}+{t}^{2}}{4-{t}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}} \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(-\\frac{t}{2\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4(4-{t}^{2})}+\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{2}+4-{t}^{2}}{4(4-{t}^{2})}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}}\\right)}=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348424 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00941107-e9c6-5933-9b3b-706a63234ba7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "38" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345358} {:answer/id #uuid "04e48a0b-e477-5875-bccf-2afaa6e18026" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345391} {:answer/id #uuid "0517197d-6ae6-5927-9dc2-64a247347fd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345394} {:answer/id #uuid "a965b668-471d-54ec-81ea-2f7db68ebe67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346828} {:answer/id #uuid "fa5975e9-7302-5301-927b-8ef8cd0130c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347561}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00941107-e9c6-5933-9b3b-706a63234ba7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "38" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345358} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e31db97-e6fd-5344-986a-bb11c57faba1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3c76c336-dd88-5200-b5c5-bdf849ab058b" :question/math-academy-id "q-80627" :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral ${∬}_{R}\\frac{2+6y{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A$,  where $R=[1,2]\\times [2,4].$" :question/worked-solution "Using the linearity property of double integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}\\frac{2+6y{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{R}(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+6y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{R}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{R}6y\\text{ }\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the first integral is independent of $y.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =(4-2){\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{x}{|}_{1}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{2}-\\left(-\\frac{2}{1}\\right)) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the second integral is independent of $x.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}6y\\text{ }\\text{d}A & =(2-1){\\int }_{2}^{4}6y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =3{y}^{2}{|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =3({4}^{2}-{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, combining our results, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}\\frac{2+6y{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A=2+36=38.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348429 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "311172a8-7e55-513e-8ad0-0a5ffe1f23c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{2},-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345785} {:answer/id #uuid "a87153a0-e08e-53d1-9b65-4f3d8b63db1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346817} {:answer/id #uuid "c99f2895-b196-5066-8383-d5366c43e803" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347107} {:answer/id #uuid "d35b0a5f-5b16-52e7-ae86-6ec97e1d9b78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{2},1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347200} {:answer/id #uuid "f4319ac2-54b4-5918-88cd-fb73b81e377e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347517}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4319ac2-54b4-5918-88cd-fb73b81e377e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347517} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a9f84a4-996e-5b49-a227-bb8512ac548b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347877}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d15295c3-5868-57e4-8227-d3f5fdb96a55" :question/math-academy-id "q-80646" :question/problem "Determine the stationary point of $f(x,y)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-4xy+{y}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The stationary points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T},$ where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is $D={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}∣x>0},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{2}{x}-4y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-4x+2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{x}-4y & -4x+2y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the stationary points, we need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x}-4y &=0 \\\\ -4x+2y &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving for $y$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{x}-4y=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{2x}.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x+2\\left(\\frac{1}{2x}\\right) & =0 \\\\ -4x+\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) & =0 \\\\ 4x & =\\frac{1}{x}. \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $y=\\frac{1}{2x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{2} & \\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}=1 \\\\ x=-\\frac{1}{2} & \\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{2\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has two solutions, $(\\frac{1}{2},1)$ and $(-\\frac{1}{2},-1).$ We reject the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},-1)$ because it does not lie in the domain of $f(x,y).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has one stationary point, $(\\frac{1}{2},1).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348435 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29ff34e9-a4f9-5efb-ad29-b203ff520685" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(2,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345726} {:answer/id #uuid "34cc476b-a22c-50b8-b73e-550217b646bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345808} {:answer/id #uuid "5b57b927-3b0b-54c7-bf20-a0a9152b21b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,1)$ and $(2,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346128} {:answer/id #uuid "773c91c3-0748-5043-8dfd-d9ad13a415b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-2)$ and $(1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346397} {:answer/id #uuid "ceb2afa7-2bcc-5d34-b559-37635169834d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,0)$ and $(2,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347160}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29ff34e9-a4f9-5efb-ad29-b203ff520685" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(2,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345726} :answer-field/id #uuid "3829a637-2a8a-579c-8d17-1ea1059a7384" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347699}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a1fd2252-93e6-52b7-b6db-b0208e66793d" :question/math-academy-id "q-80685" :question/problem "Determine the critical points of $f(x,y)={x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y+6{y}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The critical points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(a)$ does not exist, where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =3{x}^{2}-6xy \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-3{x}^{2}+12y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}^{2}-6xy & -3{x}^{2}+12y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points, we need to find the points $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ such that ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ does not exist. Note that, in our case, ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ exists everywhere, so we just need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\n\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6xy &=0 \\\\ -3{x}^{2}+12y &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving for $y$ in the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-3{x}^{2}+12y=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6x\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}\\right) & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-\\frac{6{x}^{3}}{4} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(1-\\frac{x}{2}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}(2-x) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0 & \\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{{0}^{2}}{4}=0 \\\\ x=2 & \\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{{2}^{2}}{4}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has two solutions, $(0,0)$ and $(2,1).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has two critical points, $(0,0)$ and $(2,1).$ Note that since ${f}^{′}(x)={0}^{T}$ at these two points, they are also both stationary points."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348443 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fed05df-f0a9-5182-8c0f-75ee7dd67040" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{3}\\,{\\text{e}}^{0.456\\,\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345900} {:answer/id #uuid "4195d620-a5dd-5fb6-9d35-ea652a4113bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{5}\\,{\\text{e}}^{0.817\\,\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345911} {:answer/id #uuid "9f12edf4-0568-5766-ab6f-88d3f8724787" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{3}\\,{\\text{e}}^{\\pi \\text{i}/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346740} {:answer/id #uuid "cdab0ffe-7fbd-5ec5-8741-549dae828238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{5}\\,{\\text{e}}^{\\pi \\text{i}/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347150} {:answer/id #uuid "e5353234-a4c6-5d2e-88cf-f82c2d76b215" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{5}\\,{\\text{e}}^{1.107\\,\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5353234-a4c6-5d2e-88cf-f82c2d76b215" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{5}\\,{\\text{e}}^{1.107\\,\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347369} :answer-field/id #uuid "534b7eda-89ad-556d-bfe1-28c85f1893ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347754}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "14979d9f-0bb7-56e3-a3fd-4077e5759f2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-8075" :question/problem "Write the complex number $z=1+2\\text{i}$ in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ giving $\\theta $ in radians to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "To write $z$ in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $z$ lies in the first quadrant of the complex plane, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{2}{1}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2) \\\\  & ≈1.107.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=\\sqrt{5}$ and $\\theta =1.107$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{5}\\,{\\text{e}}^{1.107\\,\\text{i}}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348446 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03d37a5d-f38e-5e9f-b4c2-8557a961d8e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{17}\\,{\\text{e}}^{6.086\\,\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345385} {:answer/id #uuid "21e41ad5-4ee1-5fe7-b639-bc9b977dc4fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{17}\\,{\\text{e}}^{3.338\\,\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345653} {:answer/id #uuid "2f556611-9499-54a3-9131-95ba2cbf06c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{26}\\,{\\text{e}}^{6.086\\,\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345767} {:answer/id #uuid "a3665dd9-b15a-5d76-8f3c-92d873804b55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{26}\\,{\\text{e}}^{3.338\\,\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346766} {:answer/id #uuid "d8ae151a-4d90-50cb-ad3c-f2ecfc64dcc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{26}\\,{\\text{e}}^{4.989\\,\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347261}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f556611-9499-54a3-9131-95ba2cbf06c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{26}\\,{\\text{e}}^{6.086\\,\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345767} :answer-field/id #uuid "e647b151-d969-5d70-9827-bf43ae96b615" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348007}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0a752e22-d98b-55fa-871d-a83c493259aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-8076" :question/problem "Write the complex number $z=5-\\text{i}$ in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ giving $\\theta $ in radians to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "To write our complex number in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{26}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant, we can find the angle $\\theta $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =2\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)| \\\\  & =2\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{-1}{5}\\right)| \\\\  & =2\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)| \\\\  & ≈2\\pi -0.197\\,39… \\\\  & ≈6.086.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=\\sqrt{26}$ and $\\theta =6.086$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{26}\\,{\\text{e}}^{6.086\\,\\text{i}}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348451 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "125a7499-daea-5b5a-bef6-ea8655c63411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=6\\,{\\text{e}}^{2\\pi \\text{i}/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345509} {:answer/id #uuid "12b079a4-9cf6-57d5-b910-bde76a8fe294" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=6\\,{\\text{e}}^{\\pi \\text{i}/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345512} {:answer/id #uuid "447bc679-763d-5ae0-afdc-316c890e6827" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=4\\,{\\text{e}}^{\\pi \\text{i}/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345942} {:answer/id #uuid "92dc2963-35f2-5f74-93b5-e7c999f47bb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=6\\,{\\text{e}}^{\\pi \\text{i}/6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346636} {:answer/id #uuid "f0ff8f7c-6d0e-56a7-ba0f-b776d57b7c1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=4\\,{\\text{e}}^{\\pi \\text{i}/6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92dc2963-35f2-5f74-93b5-e7c999f47bb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=6\\,{\\text{e}}^{\\pi \\text{i}/6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346636} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c00a6f3-3bb4-556d-900d-ebc65c30a86b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347674}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c929adfb-38d7-5819-8554-7aa009ddd6f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-8078" :question/problem "Write the complex number $z=3\\sqrt{3}+3\\text{i}$ in exponential form." :question/worked-solution "To write $z$ in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36}=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $z$ lies in the first quadrant of the complex plane, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{3\\sqrt{3}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=6$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get\n\n$$\nz=6\\,{\\text{e}}^{\\pi \\text{i}/6}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348453 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45d4c62c-2801-5936-a243-faf7482a3a8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2\\,{\\text{e}}^{5\\pi \\text{i}/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345959} {:answer/id #uuid "4d2b30e9-03f5-5b12-b9ff-13d1900f3ecc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=4\\,{\\text{e}}^{5\\pi \\text{i}/6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346013} {:answer/id #uuid "4eb24796-aaa4-5c22-a355-8f1cf7ebdbd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=4\\,{\\text{e}}^{5\\pi \\text{i}/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346022} {:answer/id #uuid "a7e5b3a1-4412-5127-9c45-53cf1c078c78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2\\,{\\text{e}}^{4\\pi \\text{i}/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346813} {:answer/id #uuid "f3c3e7c7-e11e-5f0e-8b17-1f9a3123c03c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2\\,{\\text{e}}^{5\\pi \\text{i}/6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347509}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45d4c62c-2801-5936-a243-faf7482a3a8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2\\,{\\text{e}}^{5\\pi \\text{i}/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345959} :answer-field/id #uuid "de25b2f1-9ad9-55d8-af77-b78771db89bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347996}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "df18ce8e-40b6-5c6f-8d8d-e5b04d36232b" :question/math-academy-id "q-8079" :question/problem "Write the complex number $z=1-\\text{i}\\sqrt{3}$ in exponential form." :question/worked-solution "To write our complex number in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-\\sqrt{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant, we can find the angle $\\theta $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =2\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)| \\\\  & =2\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})| \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=2$ and $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get\n\n$$\nz=2\\,{\\text{e}}^{5\\pi \\text{i}/3}.\n$$"}]
